.TH RAPPORTE 1 "2026-08-01" "scheduler" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
rapporte \- render the night's account of what this ecosystem did
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rapporte list
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B rapporte publish
\fIproject\fR \fIdated\-file\fR
.br
.B rapporte facts
.br
.B rapporte verdict
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B rapporte services
.br
.B rapporte
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rapporte
takes work that already happened and renders an account of it for a reader. It
dispatches nothing, gates nothing, and checks no file's shape.
.PP
.B Why it is not part of
.BR dose .
.B dose
discovered twenty\-five subcommands from real tooling and welded four domains
into one table: dispatch, these four accounts, the five checks now fronted by
.BR relis (1),
and the usage gate. One noun carries many verbs.
.SS What each renders
.TP
.B publish
a dated report for one project, placed where its readers look.
.TP
.B facts
the inventory of what the scheduler knows and has never printed.
.TP
.B verdict
tells NOT\-DONE from GAVE\-UP, and acts differently on each.
.TP
.B services
regenerates the browsable audit view of every registered project.
.SS morning-report is deliberately absent
.B dose
fronts
.IR bin/morning\-report.sh ,
whose own second line reads
.I "DEPRECATED (2026-07-20) \- superseded by bin/scheduler" .
A generated subcommand table cannot read a deprecation notice, so the weld
offered a retired script as a live capability. The script stays where it is
and is not offered here. Coining by hand is where that difference gets
noticed.
.SS It wraps, it does not reimplement
Every subcommand execs the existing script in scheduler's own
.IR bin/ ,
unchanged. A wrapper that quietly reimplements what it fronts is two
implementations that drift, and the drift is found by whichever one a caller
happened to use.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Not wired for any subcommand of this verb. Requesting it is reported rather
than ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the script a subcommand fronts is absent or not executable, so the
account cannot be rendered at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
Listing prints the subcommands, bare, one per line, so a caller reads the
surface without a parser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ rapporte list
list
publish
facts
verdict
services
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unknown subcommand is the caller's error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ rapporte frobnicate
rapporte: unknown subcommand: frobnicate  (try: rapporte list)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH COST
Nothing here spends money. This utility reaches no paid service, and it offers
no
.B \-\-summon
flag for any subcommand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/rapporte
This utility.
.TP
.I lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime it sources.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1),
.BR relis (1),
.BR arme (1),
.BR report (1)
.PP
Expectations transfer from
.BR dose (1)
for the domain this verb was cut out of.
.SH HISTORY
Coined 2026-08-01 with
.B bashify coin
onto a branch already carrying
.BR dose ,
.B arme
and
.BR relis ,
as the fourth verb of one noun.
